  <!-- Orthographic reference: GB/T 16159-2012, sections 6.3 and 6.5.
       Codec output: NFC, marked vowels, preserved input case, ASCII apostrophe;
       keyboard aliases are input conveniences. Internal neutral category 5
       emits no mark (external numeric notation may use 0). Retone, sandhi,
       and word segmentation require caller-supplied analysis.
       https://wsb.sjz.gov.cn/atm/7/20210601162434276.pdf -->
  <!-- Simple-vowel values only, with explicit boundaries between atoms.
       Contextual letters in ie, ei, ju, etc. require syllable analysis.
       e and yu in the syllabary below corroborate the corrected vowel values.
       Sources: Lee and Zee (2003), Standard Chinese (Beijing), JIPA 33(1),
       https://doi.org/10.1017/S0025100303001208 ; spelling/style table:
       https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Help:IPA/Mandarin . -->
  <atom spelling="a" /><atom spelling="ɤ" output="e" /><atom spelling="i" />
  <atom spelling="o" /><atom spelling="u" /><atom spelling="y" output="ü" />
  <!-- The IPA members below are curated demonstration spellings used by the
       Mandarin worked syllabifier. They combine broad spellings with selected
       phonetic detail; extensions should state their transcription convention.
       The ipa attributes supply this profile's canonical membership forms.
       Wikipedia's Mandarin IPA key guides symbol style; Lee and Zee (2003)
       is a phonetic reference. Individual rows retain their declared choices,
       including xau, ʂuei, kuɔ, and ʈ͡ʂʊŋ.
       Tone is absent because it rides the
       syllable and does not change membership. Additions belong here, at the
       Pinyin declaration boundary, rather than in Python.

       This list is a curated demonstrator with 24 declared syllables:
       syllabification is optional in this library, only the operations that
       need it depend on it, and most lexicons supply none at all. A syllable
       the list does not hold is REPORTED rather than guessed, so
       `syllabify("pʰiŋ", "mandarin")` answers with no syllables and
       `unsyllabified=((0, 3),)`, which is a legible refusal and not a silent
       wrong answer. The sibling kana declaration takes the same
       attested only position.

       It grows when a consumer needs it, and growing it is curation rather
       than pasting a table. The house pattern is the one
       `scripts/syllable_curation.py` used for the English onsets, where each
       entry carries a harvested count, an exemplar and a decision. Anything
       added here has to face where a naive table goes wrong: the syllabic
       fricatives of zhi, chi, shi, ri, zi, ci and si; the y and w spelling
       rules behind yi, wu, yu, ye and yuan; `er`; and tone riding the
       syllable without changing membership. -->
